The chief building inspector or police chief shall be and is hereby designated and appointed the public officer to exercise the powers prescribed by this article.

The public officer shall investigate and determine which buildings or structures in the city are unfit or unsafe for human habitation or occupancy. The public officer shall determine under this article, whether a dwelling, building, structure or premises is unfit or unsafe for human habitation or is unfit or unsafe for its current commercial, industrial, or business use or is a nuisance if he finds that conditions exist in such building dwelling, structure or premises, which are dangerous or injurious, to the health, safety, or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building or structure; of the occupants neighborhood dwellings, buildings or structures or of other residents of the city as determined under this article. Such conditions may include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):

- (1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;

- (2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light, or sanitary facilities; (3) — Dilapidation;

- (4) Disrepair;

- (5) Structural defects;

- (6) Uncleanliness; and

- (7) | Those conditions described in section 13-31 herein.

The public officer shall exercise such powers as may be necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the purpose and provisions of this article, including the following powers in addition to others herein granted:

- (1) Investigate the dwelling conditions in order to determine which dwellings, buildings or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or are unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use, or are vacant, dilapidated, unsafe, or are being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes;

- (2) Administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;

- (3) Enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided,

however, that such entries shall be made in such manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;

- (4) Appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agent, and employees as he deems necessary to carry out the purposes of this article; and

- (5) Delegate any of his functions and powers under this article to such officers and agents as he may designate.

- (d) The public officer designated herein may make his determination that a dwelling, building, or structure is vacant, dilapidated, and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.
